<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- iOS-ONLY additions, merged into the generated iOS bundle at BUILD time.
     Tauri looks for both `Info.plist` and `Info.ios.plist` beside tauri.conf.json;
     the shared one carries the TCC usage strings and CFBundleLocalizations for
     macOS and iOS alike, and this one carries what must NOT reach the macOS
     bundle.

     The iOS bundle registers NO custom URL scheme, and none may be added
     here. Any installed app can claim a scheme, so a scheme cannot say which
     app receives a sign-in. The sign-in sheet returns over the https address
     that iOS delivers only to the app whose `webcredentials` entitlement names
     its host, and the tests hold that address in place. Tauri does not turn
     `plugins.deep-link.mobile` into CFBundleURLTypes on iOS, so this file is
     the only place a scheme could come back from.
     Spec: docs/internal/macos-ios-setup.md (native Google sign-in / deep links) -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- iOS does not open documents, so it must not advertise that it does.
	     `bundle.fileAssociations` in tauri.conf.json exists for macOS (Apple
	     Events), Windows (registry) and Linux (MimeType), but Tauri emits it into
	     the iOS bundle too as CFBundleDocumentTypes. Nothing on iOS can act on
	     that: the Rust open handler is `#[cfg(target_os = "macos")]`, and the
	     Markdown folder capability gate returns 'unavailable' on phones because
	     the File System Access API does not exist there.

	     Transporter rejects the upload for it (error 90737, "Missing Document
	     Configuration"), which demands either UISupportsDocumentBrowser or
	     LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace. Both would be false claims: setting
	     either to YES puts PrivacyNotes in the Files app's "Open With" list for
	     every .md and .txt, where tapping it would do nothing at all. So the
	     declaration is emptied instead, which is what the app actually supports,
	     and the LS key is set false so the validator has its explicit answer
	     regardless of how it reads an empty array.

	     The suppression itself lives in `tauri.ios.conf.json`, which empties
	     `bundle.fileAssociations` for iOS only (Tauri merges a per-platform config
	     file automatically). Emptying CFBundleDocumentTypes from HERE was tried
	     first on 2026-08-15 and does NOT work: the plist merge does not replace
	     Tauri's generated array, so the entries survived. The key below stays as a
	     belt-and-braces answer for the validator in case anything ever
	     reintroduces a document type on iOS.

	     If iOS ever DOES learn to open files, delete `tauri.ios.conf.json` and the
	     key below, and let the shared fileAssociations flow through.
	     Spec: docs/internal/specs/markdown-folder-handoff.md (platform matrix) -->
	<!-- Export compliance, answered here so App Store Connect stops asking at
	     every upload. The app uses standard encryption (XChaCha20-Poly1305,
	     Ed25519, AES-GCM) beyond the OS crypto, so the answer is YES, and YES must
	     carry the key value of an APPROVED App Encryption Documentation record, or
	     Transporter rejects the upload with error 409 "Invalid Export Compliance
	     Code" (seen 2026-08-16, when no record existed). The record is the ANSSI
	     declaration, uploaded 2026-11-02 and approved (App Store Connect > App
	     Information > App Encryption Documentation). If Apple ever issues a new
	     key value, replace it here. Never set the first key to false: that claims
	     an exemption the app does not have.
	     Spec: docs/internal/tsu-notification.md (the filing, and what is still open) -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<true/>
	<key>ITSEncryptionExportComplianceCode</key>
	<string>d8a6a1dc-bd39-47e2-9dcd-1f6ab56c7afb</string>
	<!-- iOS 27 TERMINATES an app that has not adopted the scene life cycle. The
	     check is _UIApplicationEvaluateRuntimeIssueForNoSceneLifecycleAdoption and
	     it kills the process during launch, so the app never draws a frame. The
	     same binary runs on iOS 26.5, which is how the runtime was identified as
	     the trigger rather than the SDK or the build machine (2026-09-18).

	     tao already implements a scene delegate and an
	     application:configurationForConnectingSceneSession: that returns it. It
	     registers neither unless this manifest says multiple scenes are supported,
	     so the flag below is what switches tao's scene path on. It is a gate, not
	     a preference: tao reads this exact key.

	     Consequence to know about: on iPad this also permits more than one window
	     of the app. Nothing here has been exercised with two windows open.
	     Spec: docs/internal/specs/app-switcher-privacy-screen.md (section 13) -->
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
